You are an experienced Python desktop-application developer. Build a polished, maintainable GUI note-management application for todo reminders. The application must allow users to organize notes into thematic groups and track each note's status.

## Technical expectations

- Use Python 3.11+ and a mature desktop GUI toolkit. Prefer PySide6/Qt unless the existing project requires another toolkit.
- Organize the code into clear modules for the UI, models, filtering, persistence, and application startup.
- Keep the application runnable as a normal desktop program and make it straightforward to package as an executable.
- Add focused automated tests for the data model, filtering, serialization, and the required edge cases.
- Avoid losing user data: validate loaded JSON, handle malformed or missing files gracefully, and save changes atomically where practical.

## User interface

Create a single main window whose primary content is a tabbed note workspace.

### Visual design

- Use a dark theme with a dark blue background. Do not use a pure black-and-white palette.
- Use high-contrast, light pastel foreground colors for text, controls, status indicators, and selection states.
- Make the UI readable and usable with keyboard navigation, visible focus states, sensible spacing, and responsive resizing.
- Use small, consistent symbolic graphics or characters for statuses, such as a checkmark for done and an X for cancelled. Symbols must supplement, not replace, accessible text labels or tooltips.
- Keep status colors distinct and readable against the dark background; do not communicate status by color alone.

### Groups and tabs

- Each tab represents a thematic group of notes, such as "Shopping list".
- Allow the user to create, rename, delete, and reorder tabs by dragging them left or right.
- Provide discoverable controls or context-menu actions for tab operations, with confirmation before destructive deletion.
- When the final remaining tab is deleted, immediately create a replacement tab named `New Group` so the workspace is never empty.
- Preserve the selected tab and tab order when the application is restarted.

### Two-pane workspace

Each tab must contain a vertically divided two-pane layout:

- The left pane contains a scrollable list of note cards and the controls used to filter it.
- The right pane displays and edits the complete contents of the currently selected note.
- Allow the divider to be resized. Keep the selected note and its editing state clear.
- Provide actions to create and delete notes. Confirm before deleting a note.
- When no note is selected, show an intentional empty state rather than a blank or broken editor.

### Note list, filters, and search

- Represent every note as a compact two-line card:
	- First line: the note title, truncated with an ellipsis when necessary.
	- Second line: creation date and current status.
- The original outline refers to the first line as note-body text. Implement a dedicated title field so the requested "Titles only" search option is meaningful; if a legacy note has no title, derive a display title from the first line of its body.
- Provide a status filter with at least: `Todo`, `In Progress`, `Done`, and `Cancelled`.
- Provide a date filter for creation date. Include a clear `All dates` option and practical date ranges such as today, the last 7 days, the last 30 days, and a custom range if supported by the toolkit.
- Allow the status and date filters to be combined. Clearly indicate when filters are active and provide a way to clear them.
- Provide a case-insensitive text search field.
- Add a selectable checkbox labeled `Titles only`. When selected, search only the title; when unselected, search both title and note body.
- Update filtered results promptly as the user changes search text or filters. Preserve the selected note when it remains visible; otherwise select the first visible result or clear the editor.
- Show a useful empty state when no notes match the current filters.

## Notes

Each note must support:

- A title.
- A body/content field that can contain multiple lines.
- A creation timestamp.
- A last-modified timestamp.
- One status: `todo`, `in_progress`, `done`, or `cancelled`.
- A stable unique identifier.
- Creation, editing, and deletion.
- Status changes at any time, including changing a `Done` or `Cancelled` note back to an earlier status.

Status must be color-coded and also displayed as text and/or a symbol. Use a dropdown, segmented control, or equivalent clear editor control to change the status.

When a note is created, initialize its creation and modified timestamps consistently and give it the default status `todo`. Editing its title, body, or status must update the modified timestamp.

## Persistence

- Store application data in a file named `notes.json` located beside the application executable. Resolve the executable directory correctly when running from source and when packaged.
- The top-level JSON value must be an array of tab/group objects.
- Each tab object must contain a stable identifier, a name, an explicit order represented by the array position, and an array of note objects.
- Each note object must contain at least `id`, `title`, `body`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, and `status`.
- Store timestamps in an unambiguous, documented format such as ISO 8601. Display note dates in local time using the exact `dd.MM.yyyy` format, for example `28.09.2026`.
- On startup, load `notes.json` and populate tabs and notes. If the file is missing, initialize one tab named `New Group`. If the file is malformed or contains invalid records, show a clear error and recover without silently overwriting the original file.
- Automatically save after every data-changing operation. There must be no dedicated Save button.
- Data-changing operations include creating, deleting, renaming, and reordering tabs; creating and deleting notes; editing note title or body; and changing note status.
- Keep the in-memory UI and persisted data synchronized, and surface save failures to the user.

## Keyboard navigation and accessibility

- `Tab` must move between tabs/groups in a predictable way. If the toolkit's standard Tab behavior conflicts with text editing, provide a clearly documented and discoverable alternative for moving between groups while preserving normal text-field navigation.
- Up and Down arrow keys must move through the visible note list when the list has focus.
- Enter or an equivalent standard action should open/focus the selected note for editing where appropriate.
- Provide keyboard-accessible equivalents for all important actions, visible focus indicators, accessible names, and tooltips for unfamiliar icons.

## Acceptance criteria

The implementation is complete when:

1. The application starts with existing data restored from `notes.json`, or with a `New Group` tab when no valid data exists.
2. Users can create, rename, delete, and reorder groups, including the final-tab replacement behavior.
3. Users can create, edit, delete, select, and change the status of notes without status-transition restrictions.
4. Note cards show a truncated title, creation date, and readable status; the full note is editable in the right pane.
5. Status, date, and case-insensitive text filters work independently and in combination, including the `Titles only` option.
6. Every mutation is automatically persisted and survives a restart.
7. Missing, malformed, or partially invalid JSON does not crash the application or silently destroy the user's original data.
8. The core data and filtering behavior is covered by automated tests, and the project includes clear run/test instructions.

Before implementing, inspect the existing repository and preserve its conventions. Where a requirement is ambiguous, choose the simplest behavior consistent with this specification, document the decision briefly, and keep the design easy to revise.


